        "diagnosis": {
          "label": "INFEASIBLE",
          "scope": "global",
          "p_global": 0.03,
          "p_branch": 0.02,
          "reason": "The binding failure is a hard energy/voltage ceiling, independent of the sensitivity tuning. The main beam's harvested storage saturates at ~1.1 V (max_storage) because the rectified DC cannot exceed its AC open-circuit peak, measured at ~1.46 V at the globally optimal length (33 mm; 24 mm and 42 mm are far worse). The auxiliary beam is energy-isolated from storage (changing aux 18→24 mm left max_storage bit-identical) and only drives the trigger envelope. Since gate_V cannot go below 2.0 V and brownout is 1.8 V, storage can never even reach brownout, let alone boot the MCU — every tested configuration yields 0 wakes/boots/stages and 0 deliveries (faults=4). Capacitance is already at its domain floor (80 uF, highest voltage) and gate at its domain floor (2.0 V); neither length, C, gate, nor threshold can overcome the ~1.1 V ceiling. The 210/250 Hz bursts that could carry more energy are outside the main beam's reachable resonant band (its fundamental sits ~45-70 Hz across the legal length range) and arrive only as short bursts. No legal design can start the MCU, so on-time delivery is impossible. Only a small residual probability is left for unmodeled nonlinear/second-mode burst coupling that could exceed the observed peak."
        },
